Yes, but with discipline.

For **Aegis**, I recommend:

```text
MACH principles: yes, as directionally correct architecture principles.
CQRS: yes, selectively, for the parts where auditability, evidence, projections, and read models matter.
```

I would **not** recommend turning Aegis v1 into an over-distributed, event-sourced, microservice-heavy system too early.

The better formulation is:

> **Aegis should be Clean Architecture at the core, MACH-aligned at the platform boundary, and CQRS/event-log-friendly for runs, evidence, evals, memory, and outcomes.**

---

# 1. MACH Principles

MACH means:

```text
M — Microservices
A — API-first
C — Cloud-native
H — Headless
```

For Aegis, I recommend **MACH-aligned**, not necessarily “full MACH from day one.”

## M — Microservices

### Recommendation: yes logically, not physically at MVP

Aegis naturally has separate capability domains:

```text
runtime
memory
tool broker
policy
evidence
evals
feedback
outcomes
```

Those are good **service boundaries**.

But I would not deploy all of them as separate microservices in MVP or early v1.

Recommended approach:

```text
MVP:
  modular monolith or two-process system:
    aegis-api
    aegis-worker

v1:
  still likely aegis-api + aegis-worker,
  but with clean internal module boundaries

v2 / scale:
  split runtime, memory, tool-broker, policy, evidence, evals if needed
```

So yes to **microservice boundaries**, no to premature microservice deployment.

### Why

If you split too early, you pay for:

```text
distributed debugging
service discovery
network failures
cross-service transactions
deployment complexity
observability complexity
local dev pain
```

Aegis already has enough conceptual complexity. The first version should prove the control loop.

---

## A — API-first

### Recommendation: strongly yes

Aegis should absolutely be API-first.

Its whole purpose is to wrap other systems, agents, workflows, apps, and tools. That means stable contracts matter.

API-first applies to:

```text
Run API
Memory API
Tool Broker API
Policy Decision API
Evidence API
Eval API
Feedback API
Outcome API
Webhook API
SDK API
```

Aegis should define contracts before implementation:

```text
OpenAPI for HTTP APIs
JSON Schema for core objects
AsyncAPI or event specs for events
YAML manifests for tools, policies, eval packs
SDK interfaces for TypeScript/Python
```

This fits Aegis perfectly.

---

## C — Cloud-native

### Recommendation: yes, but local-first first

Aegis should be **cloud-native capable**, but **local-first by default**.

For MVP:

```text
Docker Compose
Postgres
pgvector
Redis
OPA
OTEL Collector
mock model provider
```

For v1:

```text
containerized API
containerized worker
managed Postgres option
managed Redis option
object storage
OpenTelemetry
CI/CD
secrets management
horizontal worker scaling
```

For later:

```text
Kubernetes or Nomad
Helm charts
Terraform/Pulumi
autoscaling
tenant isolation
regional deployment
```

But do not start by making Kubernetes the center of the architecture.

The center is the domain:

```text
runs
memory
tools
policy
evidence
evals
feedback
outcomes
```

Cloud-native is the deployment posture, not the product.

---

## H — Headless

### Recommendation: strongly yes

Aegis should be headless.

The Admin UI should be a client of the same APIs that SDKs and customer apps use.

That means:

```text
Admin UI should not contain business logic.
Demo Console should not contain business logic.
SDKs should not bypass policy.
External apps should integrate through the same run/tool/evidence APIs.
```

Headless is very important because Aegis may be used in multiple modes:

```text
hosted dashboard
embedded in customer workflow
SDK-only
tool-broker-only
evidence-only
evals-only
consulting delivery platform
internal AIC automation
```

So yes: Aegis should be **headless by design**.

---

# My MACH Recommendation

Use this as the architecture principle:

> **Aegis is MACH-aligned: modular service boundaries, API-first contracts, cloud-native deployability, and headless interfaces. Physical microservices are deferred until operational need justifies them.**

That is the safe version.

---

# 2. CQRS

CQRS means **Command Query Responsibility Segregation**.

The basic idea:

```text
commands change state
queries read state
```

For Aegis, I recommend **selective CQRS**, not dogmatic CQRS everywhere.

Aegis has many places where the command side and read side naturally differ.

---

# Where CQRS Fits Aegis Very Well

## 1. Runs

Commands:

```text
CreateRun
StartRun
AppendRunEvent
RequestApproval
CompleteRun
FailRun
CancelRun
```

Queries:

```text
GetRun
ListRuns
GetRunTimeline
GetRunSummary
GetRunEvidence
GetRunEvalResults
GetRunOutcomes
```

This is a great CQRS fit because the write side cares about state transitions, while the read side wants timelines, dashboards, and summaries.

