# Where I Would Not Overuse CQRS

Do not create separate command/read models for every tiny object immediately.

For example, early MVP probably does not need elaborate CQRS for:

```text
user settings
static tool manifests
basic agent config
simple lookup tables
labels/tags
small admin metadata
```

A simple repository pattern is fine there.

---

# CQRS Without Overengineering

For MVP/v1, I recommend this lightweight CQRS style:

```text
Command handlers:
  perform writes
  enforce domain rules
  append run events
  emit outbox events

Query handlers:
  read optimized views
  assemble timelines
  generate summaries
  serve dashboards
```

But keep the same database at first:

```text
Postgres write tables
Postgres read views/projections
Materialized views later if needed
Search/vector indexes where needed
```

Do **not** immediately create separate read databases unless needed.

---

# Recommended CQRS Shape

```text
packages/core/
  domain/
  application/
    commands/
      create-run.ts
      propose-tool-call.ts
      admit-memory-candidate.ts
      generate-evidence-pack.ts
    queries/
      get-run-timeline.ts
      get-evidence-summary.ts
      search-memory.ts
      get-outcome-dashboard.ts
    ports/
      run-repository.ts
      event-store.ts
      projection-store.ts
```

Example:

```text
POST /runs
  -> CreateRunCommand
  -> CreateRunHandler
  -> writes runs table
  -> writes run_events table
  -> emits run.created

GET /runs/:id/timeline
  -> GetRunTimelineQuery
  -> reads run_events projection
  -> returns timeline view
```

That gives you CQRS benefits without enterprise ceremony.

