# 3. Should Aegis Use Event Sourcing?

Related question.

I would say:

> **Use event logging from day one. Do not require full event sourcing for every aggregate in MVP.**

For Aegis, events are extremely valuable:

```text
run.created
memory.retrieved
policy.checked
tool.proposed
tool.denied
evidence.generated
eval.completed
feedback.received
outcome.recorded
```

These events power:

```text
evidence packs
run timelines
debugging
auditing
replay
analytics
eval context
business reporting
```

But full event sourcing means the event log becomes the only source of truth and all current state is rebuilt from events.

That may be too much for MVP.

Recommended approach:

```text
MVP:
  state tables + run_events table

v1:
  state tables + event log + outbox + projections

later:
  event sourcing selectively for runs if needed
```

So:

```text
Yes to event-rich architecture.
Maybe later to full event sourcing.
```

---

# 4. Recommended Architecture Formula

For Aegis, I would use this formula:

```text
Clean Architecture
+ Ports and Adapters
+ MACH-aligned boundaries
+ Selective CQRS
+ Event-rich audit trail
+ Local-first MVP
+ Cloud-native v1 readiness
```

This is stronger than saying “we use MACH” or “we use CQRS” in isolation.

---

# 5. How These Fit Together

```text
Clean Architecture:
  protects the domain core

MACH:
  shapes the external platform posture

CQRS:
  separates writes from reads where audit/projections matter

Event log:
  gives evidence, replay, audit, and timeline

Policy-as-code:
  governs runtime decisions

Ports/adapters:
  keep model/tool/database/provider choices replaceable
```

Aegis is a perfect candidate for this combination because it is inherently about controlled state transitions and inspectable activity.

